  'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p>One of the perks of renting is that you do not have to fix the furnace or replace the roof. But that does not mean renters have no maintenance responsibilities. A handful of small, regular tasks keep your apartment safe and comfortable, prevent expensive damage, and help protect your security deposit. Just as important is knowing which problems to report right away and which ones are your landlord's job to fix.</p>
<p>Here is a practical checklist for renters, with a few extra notes for Ohio's cold winters.</p>

<h2>What the Law Says (Briefly)</h2>
<p>In Ohio, landlord and tenant responsibilities are laid out in Ohio Revised Code Chapter 5321. Under section 5321.04, landlords must, among other things, keep the premises fit and habitable, make repairs needed to keep it that way, and keep electrical, plumbing, heating, and supplied appliances in good and safe working order. Under section 5321.05, tenants must keep their unit safe and sanitary, dispose of garbage properly, use fixtures and appliances reasonably, and not damage the premises.</p>
<p>Your lease may add specific responsibilities, such as changing air filters or replacing light bulbs. For a fuller overview, read our <a href="/blog/ohio-tenant-rights-guide/">Ohio tenant rights guide</a>. This article is general information, not legal advice.</p>

<h2>Monthly Tasks</h2>
<h3>Test smoke and carbon monoxide detectors</h3>
<p>Press the test button on each detector once a month. If an alarm chirps, it usually means the battery is low. Replace it promptly, or tell maintenance if the detector is hardwired or sealed. Never disconnect or remove a detector. If your unit has fuel-burning appliances like a gas stove or furnace and you do not see a carbon monoxide detector, ask your landlord about one.</p>
<h3>Clean drains and prevent clogs</h3>
<p>Use drain catchers in the shower and tub, keep grease out of the kitchen sink, and only flush toilet paper. Most clogs are preventable, and some leases charge tenants for clogs caused by misuse.</p>
<h3>Check under sinks</h3>
<p>Take a quick look under kitchen and bathroom sinks for drips, water stains, or a musty smell. Catching a slow leak early prevents mold and cabinet damage.</p>
<h3>Wipe down the range hood filter</h3>
<p>Grease builds up quickly. Many range hood filters can be removed and washed in hot, soapy water. This also reduces fire risk.</p>

<h2>Every Few Months</h2>
<h3>Replace or check the HVAC filter</h3>
<p>If your lease makes filters your responsibility, change them on the schedule the filter or your landlord recommends. A clogged filter makes your system work harder, which can raise energy bills. Our guide to <a href="/blog/lower-utility-bills-apartment-ohio/">lowering utility bills in Ohio apartments</a> has more efficiency tips.</p>
<h3>Clean the dryer lint trap and check the vent</h3>
<p>Clean the lint trap after every load. If clothes are taking longer to dry, the vent may be clogged, which is a fire hazard. Report it to maintenance.</p>
<h3>Deep clean the refrigerator and dishwasher</h3>
<p>Wipe out spills, toss old food, and clean the dishwasher filter if it has one. This keeps appliances working well and smelling fresh.</p>
<h3>Look for signs of pests</h3>
<p>Droppings, chewed packaging, or insects near baseboards should be reported promptly. Early treatment is easier for everyone.</p>

<!--products-->

<h2>Seasonal Tasks for Ohio Winters</h2>
<p>West Carrollton winters regularly bring freezing temperatures, and frozen pipes are one of the most damaging and avoidable apartment problems.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Keep the heat on.</strong> Never turn the heat completely off, even if you are away for the holidays. Ask your landlord what minimum temperature they recommend.</li>
<li><strong>Open cabinet doors.</strong> On very cold nights, open sink cabinets on exterior walls so warm air can reach the pipes.</li>
<li><strong>Drip faucets if advised.</strong> During extreme cold, a slow drip can help prevent freezing. Follow your landlord's guidance.</li>
<li><strong>Disconnect outdoor hoses.</strong> If you have a patio spigot, remove the hose before the first freeze.</li>
<li><strong>Seal drafts.</strong> Use draft stoppers on doors and report windows that do not close or seal properly.</li>
<li><strong>Report heat problems immediately.</strong> A lack of heat in winter is urgent. Call the emergency maintenance line.</li>
</ul>
<p>For a full seasonal routine, see our <a href="/blog/seasonal-apartment-cleaning-ohio/">seasonal apartment cleaning guide</a>.</p>

<h2>Report These Right Away</h2>
<p>Some problems should never wait. Report them as soon as you notice them, by phone for emergencies and in writing so there is a record.</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Water leaks</strong> from ceilings, pipes, appliances, or the water heater.</li>
<li><strong>No heat</strong> in cold weather, or no air conditioning if it is provided and conditions are dangerous.</li>
<li><strong>Gas smell.</strong> Leave the unit immediately and call the gas utility's emergency line or 911 from outside, then notify your landlord.</li>
<li><strong>Electrical issues</strong> like sparking outlets, burning smells, or breakers that trip repeatedly.</li>
<li><strong>Mold</strong> that appears or spreads, especially after a leak.</li>
<li><strong>Broken locks, doors, or windows</strong> that affect security.</li>
<li><strong>Sewage backups</strong> or toilets that will not stop running or overflowing.</li>
</ol>
<p>Prompt reporting matters. Delaying can make damage worse, and in some cases you may be held responsible for damage that grew because a problem was not reported. Take dated photos for your records.</p>

<h2>What Is Usually the Landlord's Job</h2>
<table>
<thead>
<tr><th>Typically Tenant</th><th>Typically Landlord</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Testing detectors, replacing accessible batteries</td><td>Installing and replacing faulty detectors</td></tr>
<tr><td>Keeping drains clear of hair and grease</td><td>Repairing plumbing and fixing leaks</td></tr>
<tr><td>Changing filters if the lease says so</td><td>Repairing and servicing the HVAC system</td></tr>
<tr><td>Reporting problems promptly</td><td>Making repairs to keep the unit habitable</td></tr>
<tr><td>Keeping the unit clean and sanitary</td><td>Maintaining common areas and supplied appliances</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Your lease is the final word on specifics, so read it carefully.</p>

<h2>If Repairs Are Not Made</h2>
<p>If you have reported a problem in writing and your landlord does not fix it within a reasonable time, Ohio law provides options. Under Ohio Revised Code 5321.07, a tenant who is current on rent and has given written notice may, after a reasonable time (not more than 30 days) passes, deposit rent with the clerk of the local municipal or county court rather than paying the landlord. This process has specific requirements, so read the statute or talk to a local legal aid organization before using it. This is not legal advice.</p>

<h2>Small Habits, Big Payoff</h2>
<p>A few minutes a month on these tasks keeps your apartment safer, helps you avoid move-out charges, and makes it easier to work with your landlord when something does go wrong. Pair this checklist with good renters insurance, and you will be prepared for most surprises. Our <a href="/blog/renters-insurance-claims-guide/">renters insurance claims guide</a> explains what to do if something serious happens.</p>
